Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Cicadas Molt in the Pressure Chamber
- Story & Context: In the spring of 1959 at the North Atlantic cable relay terminal in Valentia, engineer Marcus Finch locked himself in the pressure-test vault with an accordion and a reel of copper wire to process the strange telemetry coming off the ocean floor. As the tape machine overheated, the rhythmic scraping of the accordion reeds began to mirror the frantic clicking of emerging cicadas, drowning out the alarms as the pressure gauges spiked into the red. Finch recorded the entire collapse, leaning into the microphone to whisper the now-famous hook, "Listen close as the shell splits wide, we are breathing in the salt tide." The track captures this literal origin, opening with real hydrophone hum before erupting into a towering 11/4 post-rock crescendo.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic album cover art showing a translucent, mechanical cicada shedding its golden exoskeleton inside a rusted 1950s brass deep-sea diving helmet resting on the ocean floor. Glowing copper transatlantic data cables thread through the helmet's viewport, emitting faint blue electrical sparks into murky dark blue sea water. Atmospheric macro photography, cinematic lighting, ultra-detailed, eerie sci-fi art.

Track 2: Krasnogorsk-5 Swarms the Transatlantic Line
- Story & Context: Named after the Soviet spring-wound movie camera used by coastal smugglers to film hidden underwater cable routes, this track tells the story of Alina Vane, a disaffected radio operator who intercepted classified signals from the seabed. Sitting in her damp cabin overlooking the Dover straits, she translated the encrypted Morse code bursts into melancholic accordion melodies, matching the mechanical wind-up click of her camera to the tempo of the sea. During a stormy night shift, she broadcast her musical decoding across open civilian frequencies, singing softly into the static, "Every frame takes a piece of the dark, leaving only our faint spark." The song operates as a brooding character study, driven by a melancholic Wurlitzer piano line that slowly transforms into an orbital wall of symphonic feedback.
